# 8. LOG COLLECTION

The SIEM receives logs from different systems.

Example:

```text
Windows Event Logs
Linux auth logs
Firewall logs
DNS logs
EDR telemetry
```

Collection methods can include:

- Agents
- Syslog
- APIs
- Network collectors
- Cloud integrations
- File-based ingestion

---

# 9. LOG NORMALIZATION

Different systems use different formats.

Example:

```text
Windows:
4625

Firewall:
DENY

Linux:
authentication failure
```

SIEM normalization can transform these into common fields such as:

```text
timestamp
username
source_ip
destination_ip
event_type
action
status
```

---

# 10. WHY NORMALIZATION MATTERS

Without normalization:

```text
Windows format
Linux format
Firewall format
      ↓
Hard to compare
```

With normalization:

```text
Different sources
      ↓
Common fields
      ↓
Easy correlation
```

---

# 11. CORRELATION

Correlation connects related events.

Example:

```text
10:00 — 10 failed logins
10:02 — Successful login
10:04 — Privileged action
10:05 — Large outbound connection
```

Individually:

```text
Failed login → Maybe normal
Successful login → Normal
Privileged action → Maybe normal
Outbound traffic → Maybe normal
```

Together:

> **Potential compromise**

---

# 12. SIMPLE CORRELATION RULE

Example:

```text
IF
    failed_logins > 10
AND
    successful_login occurs
AND
    source_ip is unusual
THEN
    generate high-priority alert
```

This is a simplified example.

